Z||000000||fierofreek2001||01-07-2001||09:03 PM||||heres what i want to do.. i want to take an 85 v6, attach it to a 84 4 cyl automatic and transaxle, and put it in my 85 4cyl car<BR>i know theres some different gear ratios in some of the automatic transaxles like the difference between an se and a gt<BR>also..is the bolt pattern on the back of the v6 and 4 cyl. the same? <BR>will all the wiring harnesses and computer harnesses for the v6 , when i exchange them ,work in the 4 cylinder car? we have a complete v6 85 fiero, but it is a stick. <BR>is there a form online that would explain what fits what and where and why?  basically, the goal is to have a v6 automatic in my 85 <BR>thanks everyone<BR>steve <A HREF="http://www.fierosteve.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierosteve.com</A>  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Will||01-07-2001||10:49 PM||||The bellhousing bolt patterns are the same. You will need the V6 wiring harness, computer, and fuel pump (the TPI V6 requires much more fuel pressure than the TBI Iron Duck). Why do you want an automatic? They're called slushboxes for a reason...<P>My experience has been going the other way: auto to stick conversions.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||01-08-2001||02:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>...and fuel pump (the TPI V6 requires much more fuel pressure than the TBI Iron Duck)...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually stock Fiero V6's have MFI, not TPI.<BR>But as Will says, they still need the higher pressure fuel pump. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||01-08-2001||03:28 AM||||Stock V6's have M<B>P</B>FI  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Multi-Point Fuel Injection||||reg||
Z||000004||Will||01-08-2001||10:43 AM||||What's the difference between a TPI and what the Fiero has? Just the number of cylinders.<BR>Both setups have port injectors fired in batches; both setups have long tuned length runners. There really isn't much conceptual difference between the two setups. They just called it TPI because it started as the hi po Crovette injection and had to have a catchy name.<BR>Call it what you like, but I'll call it TPI.<BR>||||reg||1
